// expect: 23
// A `let` whose pattern takes every value never refuses, so its refusal arm
// can never run and charges nothing: a tuple destructure stays `Pure`. The
// arm is still there (the desugar appends it to every destructuring let);
// what keeps the row clean is coverage marking it dead, and a dead arm
// performing nothing. Were the arm judged live, `abort()` would put `Abort`
// in the row and the declaration would refuse.
fn weigh(p) with Pure = {
  let (a, b) = p
  a * 10 + b
}

fn main() = weigh((2, 3))
